@@ -0,0 +1,235 @@
+# SettingsUI
+
+Native settings controls and adaptive layouts for macOS 26, built with Swift 6.2.
+The package owns presentation. Your app supplies values, bindings, selection,
+and actions, and owns persistence and runtime effects.
+
+## Add the package
+
+Add this directory as a local Swift package in Xcode, or declare it in your
+package manifest:
+
+```swift
+dependencies: [.package(path: "../SettingsUI")]
+// In the consuming target:
+dependencies: [.product(name: "SettingsUI", package: "SettingsUI")]
+```
+
+With XcodeGen, use a local package and its library product:
+
+```yaml
+packages:
+  SettingsUI:
+    path: ../SettingsUI
+# Under your application target:
+dependencies:
+  - package: SettingsUI
+    product: SettingsUI
+```
+
+This is an unreleased local package. There is no hosted repository URL or
+published semantic version yet. Copy the complete package directory or use a
+shared checkout. Its only external dependency is ColorTokensKit-Swift, pinned
+to 0.3.0. The standalone example is a separate consumer and can be copied with
+the package while preserving its relative dependency path.
+
+## Build a settings page
+
+```swift
+import SettingsUI
+import SwiftUI
+
+struct GeneralSettings: View {
+    @Binding var enabled: Bool
+
+    var body: some View {
+        SSectionCard(title: "General") {
+            SToggle("Enable notifications", isOn: $enabled)
+        }
+        .padding(SettingsSpacing.lg)
+        .settingsTheme(.standard)
+        .settingsFontScale(1)
+    }
+}
+```
+
+Pass bindings from your existing settings owner. Do not create a second settings
+store to adapt the controls. The library does not read UserDefaults or start
+application services.
+
+## Tune the example
+
+Run `Examples/SettingsUIExample/scripts/run-example.sh` from the repository root.
+Use Colors, Surfaces, Background, and Motion to edit the shared design, then
+export the preset from the Preset page. The file dialog starts in `exports/`.
+The example has its own bundle identity and does not use Radial Launcher's
+preferences. Its README describes the current tuning controls and export path.
+
+Construct a `SettingsTheme` with the desired semantic colors or surface values,
+using its initializer defaults for unchanged roles. Inject it with
+`.settingsTheme(theme)` at the
+window root. The theme is an environment value, so separate windows can use
+independent appearances. Text scale is injected separately with
+`.settingsFontScale(scale)`.
+
+### Export a style preset
+
+The example's Preset page edits separate light and dark designs. Auto follows
+the current macOS appearance and edits the active design; choosing Light or Dark
+forces that preview and edits only that design. The example auto-saves its draft
+locally and imports or exports both designs as one versioned JSON file.
+
+Decode a preset owned by a host app with `SettingsStylePreset.decodeJSON(_:)`.
+The preset contains one shared `brand` recipe and separate light and dark
+appearance records for surfaces, backdrop, and motion. Resolve the selected
+appearance with `appearance.theme(brand: preset.brand)`, then pass its other
+values to window chrome and backdrop components. The host still owns where the
+file is stored and whether users may change these values.
+
+Generated brand foregrounds are selected after panel and card colors are
+alpha-composited over their configured backgrounds. This keeps opacity changes
+readable in the deterministic palette model. Native material and vibrancy sample
+a live backdrop, so the host should still verify those surfaces in the running
+window and with macOS accessibility display options.
+
+Keep app-specific window dimensions, preview sizes, autosave timing, and settings
+defaults in the host app. Package defaults describe shared presentation.
+
+To adopt the package and an exported preset in another app, follow
+[Adopt SettingsUI in an app](docs/adopting-settingsui.md). The guide includes
+the package dependency, preset loading, appearance resolution, user override
+ownership, and a verification checklist.
+
+## Components and ownership
+
+| API | Purpose |
+| --- | --- |
+| `SSectionCard`, `SMetricCard` | Groups and summaries with semantic status tones |
+| `SToggle`, `SPicker`, `SSegmentedControl`, `STextField`, `SSlider` | Binding-driven setting controls |
+| `SHelp`, `SInfo`, `SLabeledControl` | Descriptions, default labels, and custom controls |
+| `SInventoryRow` | Selection and hover treatment for inventories |
+| `SettingsCategoryBar` | Horizontal category choices with a menu fallback |
+| `SettingsWorkspace` | Stable inventory and detail identity across column and drawer layouts |
+| `SettingsColumnResizeHandle` | Bounded pointer, keyboard, and accessibility width adjustment |
+| `SettingsBackdrop` | Decorative animation with window focus, visibility, and Reduce Motion gating |
+
+`SettingsCategoryBar` receives your category models and selection binding. The
+host normalizes selection when its available categories change.
+
+`SettingsWorkspace` receives an explicit stacking decision, page identity, width
+binding, inventory builder, and detail builder. Compute stacking from the space
+the host can offer. Changing arrangement keeps the children mounted; changing
+page identity deliberately replaces page-local state. Keep selection and drafts
+in the host when they should survive page changes.
+
+`SSlider` supports debounced and drag-end commit modes. Keyboard changes in
+drag-end mode commit directly. Choose the
+mode to match the setting's runtime cost. The library does not infer whether a
+setting requires service reconfiguration.
+
+`SettingsBackdrop` owns its own timeline. Pass `isInteractionEngaged` when the
+host needs animation paused during another operation. No animation phase is
+published to the settings page or its store.
+
+## Accessibility
+
+Shared inputs expose their setting title to accessibility clients. Segmented
+controls require a title even though their visual label stays hidden. Sliders
+expose their formatted value, inventory rows expose selection, and section cards
+contain independently accessible controls. Row help buttons include the setting
+name; standalone `SInfo` callers should supply a contextual `title`. Decorative
+backdrops do not participate in accessibility or pointer hit testing.
+
+Give custom controls inside `SLabeledControl` their own meaningful accessibility
+labels, especially when a row contains several actions. Do not combine a whole
+page into one accessibility element. Verify the resulting native accessibility
+tree and keyboard actions in the host app; a successful build does not prove
+assistive technology behavior.
+
+## Verify an integration
+
+Run `swift test` from the repository root and
+build the example with `Examples/SettingsUIExample/scripts/run-example.sh --no-launch`.
+Then build the host app through its normal project generator and build route.
+
+Check the actual window at narrow and wide sizes, with larger text, both
+appearances, keyboard navigation, and macOS accessibility settings. Resize while
+editing and verify selection, drafts, focus, and disclosure state. Exercise
+saved values through the host's normal relaunch path. Scheduling tests prove
+animation gating, not frame rate or rendering cost.
+
+The companion `$settings-ui` skill guides adoption using this installed package
+and example as the API reference. Its canonical local source is
+`~/.agents/skills/settings-ui/SKILL.md`; Codex and Claude discover symlinks to
+that source. Copy that skill folder to another machine's canonical skill home
+alongside the package checkout. The skill carries no application code and does
+not install dependencies automatically.
+
+## Editable glow backdrops
+
+Pass `glows: [SettingsBackdropGlow]` to `SettingsBackdrop` to supply a custom
+palette. Each glow has a stable unique ID, color, normalized x/y position,
+normalized radius relative to the longest edge, and opacity intensity. The
+renderer bounds coordinates and opacity to 0...1 and radius to 0.05...1.5;
+nonfinite values use the lower bound. An empty array renders no glows. Omitting
+the argument retains Radial's original appearance-dependent backdrop. The same
+window lifecycle and Reduce Motion rules apply to custom glows.
+
+The example's Backdrop page provides draggable points, native color pickers,
+position/spread/intensity sliders, reset, and a selectable Swift configuration.
+The host owns this configuration; the package does not persist it.
+
+`SettingsBackdropFinish` controls glow opacity, saturation, grain, and vignette.
+Pass it through `SettingsBackdrop(finish:)`. Defaults preserve the unfiltered
+rendering. Grain uses a full-area Core Image noise render retained per backdrop. Soft-light
+blending modulates the existing backdrop color instead of painting gray flecks. It never
+tiles or regenerates for animation ticks. Resizing crops the retained image;
+growth beyond its bounds renders a larger image in 256-point increments.
+The modifier decorates only the backdrop; controls remain outside its filters.
+Reduce Transparency suppresses grain and vignette. Values are bounded when
+rendered. The example exports finish values alongside its glow configuration.
+
+### Symbol alignment
+
+Use `settingsSymbol()` for standalone navigation and toolbar symbols and
+`SettingsIconLabelStyle()` for icon-and-text labels. Both use a 16-point medium
+SF Symbol in a 24-point square slot, scaled with the settings font scale. Keep
+the button hit target separate from the symbol slot. Inventory rows, category
+buttons, and workspace disclosure controls use this shared geometry. Compact
+navigation centers the slot and removes label spacing; expanded navigation
+aligns the slot on the leading edge. Do not resize SF Symbols into arbitrary
+rectangles or inherit their size from nearby headings.
+
+### Window chrome
+
+Place `SettingsWindowChrome(glassEnabled:tintOpacity:)` once at the back of the
+window root. It uses AppKit behind-window `underWindowBackground` material,
+with the theme background as a tint. It owns the clear window canvas while
+mounted and restores the original canvas on removal. Keep opaque page panels
+above it for readability. Reduce Transparency always resolves to solid chrome.
+This is classic AppKit material, not full-window Liquid Glass.
+
+Card and control opacity affect the complete decorative background, including
+material, tint, border, and shadow. Tint opacity remains an independent theme
+parameter. Text and interactive content do not fade. Reduce Transparency keeps
+the opaque fallback regardless of decorative opacity.
+
+Backdrop animation accepts speed and update-limit settings and an optional
+`onTimelineUpdate` callback. It reports delivered TimelineView dates, not GPU
+frames or full-view invalidations. Leave the callback nil outside diagnostics.
+`SettingsWindowChrome` accepts an optional color scheme; nil clears the window
+appearance override and follows macOS. It restores the original override when
+detached. Workspace arrangement and drawer changes animate with Reduce Motion
+support; continuous divider drags do not receive implicit animations.
+
+`SettingsSidebarFooter` gives pinned sidebar utilities a subtle separator and
+consistent spacing. `SettingsAppearancePicker` provides icon choices for system,
+light, and dark appearance, with a compact menu variant. The host owns its
+`SettingsAppearance` selection and applies its optional `colorScheme` to the window.
+
+### Palette experiments
+
+`SettingsPaletteConfiguration` builds opt-in OKLCH palettes with sRGB gamut
+mapping and foreground contrast selection for both appearances. See
+[the color-system inventory and rationale](docs/color-system.md).
+`SettingsThemeColorToken` enumerates all 38 stored theme colors.
